  "selection_relevant_findings": [
    "Original rowTuple C1 performs state/start/geometry calls. C8 executes eight unrolled triplets, 24 hot calls. The geometry return is discarded before payload next-rank load; this is avoidable aggregate-get consumer work, not required geometry semantics.",
    "Native planes and Series C8 have zero hot helper calls. They retain visible native prefix logic in the consumer; rowTuple start performs prepared-control dispatch, sixteen strided halfword loads assembled into lanes, shuffle/shift-mask and widened prefix reduction. No hardware gather instruction occurs in the inspected bodies.",
    "RowTuple completes each helper triplet and issues that chain payload load before the next triplet. This exposes ordinary call/ABI boundaries in independent-chain work; emitted scheduling alone does not establish the measured timing cause.",
    "RowTuple C8 caller frame is comparable to native/Series frames, not uniquely larger. ARM prefix helper only saves 16 B; no decoded-width stack array. X86 selected grain-2 path genuinely spills one stride-derived 8 B address term at post-save RSP-72, after 48 B pushes: deepest observed address 120 B below helper entry. This is not 72 B of traffic or a decoded geometry buffer.",
    "X86 rowTuple C1/C8 drivers normalize identically v3/v4 after relocations/targets. V4 rowTuple helper uses Boolean vpternlogd folds, retains short-load/lane-insert/shuffle/multiply route, and shrinks four static instructions. No new AVX-512 gather or VBMI route is used.",
    "V4 native-plane C8 changes to full eight-chain unroll from rolled lane loops in v3 (Zen paired lanes, Granite single lanes). Series stays rolled. ISA-profile timing differences therefore include compiler scheduling/register-allocation changes."
  ],
